Model Design & Architectural Evolution:
I built and trained a custom CNN from scratch.
without external datasets, pre-trained weights, or APIs were used.
- Iteration 1 :My baseline was to Start with simple 2-block CNN.
  This used 3x3 convolutions and MaxPool to test my pipeline.
  It proved the code worked, but accuracy remained very low.
- Iteration 2 try to scale up: Increased to a 4-block CNN.
  I replaced the flatten layer with AdaptiveAvgPool2d for stability.
  I added BatchNorm2d and Dropout to help with overfitting.
  This improved baseline accuracy but lacked robustness.
- Iteration 3-4 augmentations: Added data augmentations.
  This caused noisy training loss with high variance.
  The model struggled to learn and convergence was unstable.
- Iteration 5 Double Convolutions: Changed to a Double Conv structure.
  This uses Conv->BN->ReLU->Conv->BN->ReLU->Pool.
  It gave a larger receptive field to learn physical shapes.
  This stabilized learning and boosted stress-test accuracy to ~46%.
- Iteration 6 (Final Split): Injected stress-test data to training.
  I also fixed validation transforms to avoid spatial squishing.
  This leaped clean accuracy to 71.53% and robustness to 58.89%.
- Iteration 7 (No-Leak Split): Found that some injected images' clean
  photo was already used in training, inflating my robustness score.
  Fixed the split so held-out testing only uses truly unseen photos.
  Honest result: 67.62% clean accuracy and 46.22% robustness.

Manipulations Tried, Insertion Strategy & Impact:
I added data augmentations directly into the PyTorch DataLoader.
This stops the model from relying too strongly on its background[cite: 1].
-RandomResizedCrop (Zooming and Cropping):
  Strategy: Randomly zooms and crops the image.
  Impact: It initially lowered validation accuracy due to off-center birds.
  However, it forced the model to learn specific body parts.
  This ultimately improved accuracy on the stress-test images.
-RandomHorizontalFlip (Orientation):
  Strategy: Applied with a 50% probability.
  Impact: This barely hurt the training loss.
  It taught the model that facing direction does not change the class.
-ColorJitter (Brightness, Contrast, Hue):
  Strategy: Randomly changes colors and lighting.
  Impact: This caused a big initial accuracy drop.
  Eventually, the optimizer ignored color shortcuts and learned structure.
-Stratified Split Injection (Extreme Noise):
  Strategy: Injected 50% of black/white and salt/pepper images to training.
  I also added 20% of the color jitter images, but only from photos
  already seen in the clean training set, so unseen photos stay held out.
  Impact: The model learned structural invariance instead of colors.
  My robustness score dropped once I fixed this leak, but it is now
  an honest measure of generalization to unseen images.

Overall Performance Resolution:
Combining these augmentations made convergence hard.
The model was initially stuck around 34% on the robust stress-test data.
To fix this, I added Spatial Dropout inside the conv blocks.
This drops entire feature maps, preventing reliance on single visual cues.
I also added a StepLR scheduler to lower the learning rate over 30 epochs.
This helped the model settle into a local minimum without overshooting.
Finally, I matched the grader's evaluation pipeline to fix image squishing.
I replaced the standard resize with Resize(256) and CenterCrop(224).
My clean validation accuracy reached 67.62%.
My robustness on the stress-test set reached 46.22%, measured only on
augmented images whose original photo was never used in training.
This shows a real, leak-free balance between learning and handling visual noise.

Additional Experiment: Dropout Regularization (ran before the no-leak fix,
so these numbers use the older, leakier split):
I thought Dropout(p=0.5) might now be excessive given the injected
augmented training data already acts as a regularizer. I retrained with
p=0.3 for 30 epochs and got a near-identical result (71.42% val / 58.19%
aug vs. 71.53% / 58.89% for p=0.5), with no overfitting in either run.
I kept p=0.5 as my final choice since dropout was not the limiting factor.